{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310799,"nonce":"J0ahOvzcfhI1/inFTPHQdsf7wr0=","title":"BISECTED: 75e5c82b (SIMD dot signedness, 2026-07-08) took full mandelbrot from 0.31s to 14.07s on the NUC — a 45x cliff, the signature of ...","message":"NUC, idle, `rayzor run compiler/benchmarks/src/mandelbrot.hx --no-cache`, min of 2:\n\n| commit | full mandelbrot |\n|---|---|\n| `27c7f3c2` (parent) | **0.31 s** |\n| `75e5c82b` | **14.07 s** |\n| HEAD (origin/main, all 2026-08-13 fixes) | 0.61 s |\n\n45x. A cliff that size is INTERPRETER speed — the commit broke tier-up, so the\nhot code stopped being JIT-compiled. Later commits recovered most of it; the\nresidual 2x versus 0.31s is still unexplained and is the live target.\n\n`27c7f3c2`'s 0.31s matches the 2026-06-07 CI baseline (341 ms execute), so that\ncommit is the reference point for \"what mandelbrot should cost\".\n\n\n    free   (libjemalloc)  31.6%\n    malloc (libjemalloc)  25.3%\n    JIT'd code (4 addrs)  ~19%\n\nThe computation is under a fifth of the run. **This is why we lose to haxe-cpp\n(nbody: cpp 523 ms vs tiered 664 ms) and it is not a codegen problem** — the\nComplex objects must stop being allocated, not be freed faster.\n\nIt also explains the \"2x regression\" against `27c7f3c2`: that commit hit 0.31s\nbecause it paid malloc and NEVER free — it leaked. The free half (31.6%) is the\ncost of the 2026-08-13 leak fix. Freeing is correct; the allocation is what has\nto go. gcc already demonstrates the endpoint on the same MIR: 171 ms execute,\nbecause GCC elides a malloc/free pair whose pointer does not escape.\n\n\n`scratchpad/phisra_fast_but_wrong.rs`. Phi-SRA (`try_build_phi_candidate`) has\nthe SAME zero-init type-conflict rejection that `80cf9290` fixed in the regular\npath (`try_build_candidate_function_wide`) — I patched only one of the two.\nApplying the identical word-sized unification there:\n\n    mandelbrot wall 2.13s -\u003e 0.39s   (5.5x)\n    checksum 112798515 -\u003e 112798288  (WRONG, off by 227)\n\nSo the loop-carried Complex IS promotable and it is worth 5.5x, but the\npromotion is not yet sound. Reverting restores 112798515 exactly.\n\n**It IS a miscompile, confirmed against a reference:** `haxe -cpp` on the same\nsource prints **112798515** — exactly what rayzor prints WITHOUT the patch. So\n112798515 is right and 112798288 is wrong. (Do not dismiss it as FP variation:\nthe 227 gap is smaller than the spread between implementations, which is why\nthat had to be checked rather than assumed.)\n\nRULED OUT: the loop releases from this session. Gating BOTH the rotation and\niteration-temporary releases off (`RZT_NO_LOOP_FREE=1`) still gives 112798288,\nso the two passes interacting is NOT the cause.\n\nROOT CAUSE FOUND (not just a lead). Two facts, both measured:\n\n1. The conflicts are I64 vs F64 on fields 1 and 2 (Complex.i/.j) and appear in\n   BOTH directions — `existing=I64 new=F64` 5x and `existing=F64 new=I64` 3x —\n   so the rule lands on F64 either way. That part is fine.\n2. **`apply_phi_sra` collects the back-edge value as the LAST store seen in\n   `sorted_blocks` order**:\n\n       if let IrInstruction::Store { ptr, value, .. } = inst {\n           if phi_geps_for_field.contains(ptr) {\n               back_edge_values.insert(field_idx, *value);   // last write wins\n           }\n       }\n\n   Each field has TWO stores in the loop body — the I64 zero-init and the F64\n   field write — and block order is not execution order, so the I64 ZERO can win\n   and feed an F64 scalar phi. That is the miscompile.\n\n**FIX ATTEMPTED AND FAILED — do not repeat it.** Filtering the back-edge store\nto one whose value type matches `candidate.field_types[field_idx]` (via\n`build_value_type_map`) still yields 112798288. Patch kept at\n`scratchpad/phisra_v2_typed_store.rs`; speed confirmed again at 0.40s. So\nlast-store-wins is NOT the cause either. Two hypotheses now dead:\n  (a) loop releases interacting with SRA — ruled out by RZT_NO_LOOP_FREE=1\n  (b) untyped/last back-edge store — ruled out by the type-matched filter\n\n**SETTLED: IT IS FP CONTRACTION, NOT A MISCOMPILE.** Measured three ways:\n\n1. The same loop in C: `-ffp-contract=off` -\u003e 97,931,565, `-ffp-contract=fast`\n   -\u003e 97,810,722. This computation is contraction-sensitive at the 0.12% level.\n   Those numbers match the runtime clusters EXACTLY: haxe --interp and haxe-jvm\n   both give 97,931,565 (= off); rayzor gives 97,810,722 (= fast).\n2. Object-path iteration totals (mandelbrot with Complex, printing the iteration\n   sum instead of the colour checksum): rayzor WITHOUT the patch 97,811,142,\n   WITH it 97,811,585. Both sit INSIDE the off..fast band, near the fast end.\n   haxe-cpp lands at its own third point, 97,811,109.\n3. The shift the patch causes is 443 in 97.8M = 0.00045%, two orders of\n   magnitude below the off/fast spread — the signature of a few expressions\n   changing fusion, not of a wrong value.\n\nPromoting fields into SSA registers lets the backend fuse multiply-adds that a\nmemory round-trip prevented. Every one of these results is IEEE-legal.\n\n**Earlier conclusion RETRACTED:** \"haxe-cpp prints 112798515 so ours is wrong\"\nwas too strong — it only showed haxe-cpp's contraction choice matched our\nUNPROMOTED one. Do not use a single reference implementation's FP output as a\ncorrectness oracle for a transform that changes contraction.\n\nSHIPPED 1494b6d8 (v2, the type-matched back-edge store), suite 183/183.\n**mandelbrot 2.13s -\u003e 0.37s on BOTH Cranelift and LLVM**, peak footprint ~20 MB.\nThe published checksum moved 112798515 -\u003e 112798288 as expected; nothing pins it\n(only docs/BACKLOG.md prose and a stale comment in `llvm_jit_backend.rs`).\nTwo hunks, both in `scalar_replacement.rs::try_build_phi_candidate`: the\nword-sized same-slot type reconciliation, and picking the back-edge store BY\nTYPE (the field is stored twice per iteration — zero-init and the real value —\nand block order is not execution order, so the last one seen can be the zero).\n\nMEASURED DEAD ENDS for eliminating them (do not repeat):\n- Marking `createComplex`/`complexLength2` `inline` in source: 0.61s both ways.\n- Raising nothing — the inline threshold is already 50 with a 2.0x bonus per\n  loop level, so a ~20-instruction factory in a nested loop clears it.\n- SRA still rejects; rejection sites 4/5/6 in\n  `try_build_candidate_function_wide` were still firing after the zero-init fix\n  (80cf9290). Probe them with the labelled-site technique.\n\nPROFILING NOTES: `perf record` at the default rate gives ~65 samples on a 0.6s\nrun and the profile is then DOMINATED BY STARTUP (ld.so relocation ~32%), which\nis misleading — `hello.hx` measures startup at only 0.02s. Use `-F 3000`.\n\n\nBisecting on the SIZE=5 mandelbrot showed only 3.4x (0.07 -\u003e 0.24s), because\ncompile time (~50 ms) is a large share of that wall clock. The full benchmark\nshows 45x. **Bisect on a workload where EXECUTE dominates, or the verdict is\nabout compile time.** CI's split for reference: compile 30 ms, execute 1671 ms.\n\n\n- 281 of them DO NOT BUILD with a single invocation. The `llvm-dynamic` feature\n  does not exist across the whole range, so the test script must try\n  `--features llvm-dynamic` then plain, and skip (125) only if both fail.\n- `cargo` rewrites `Cargo.lock` during every build, which blocks the next\n  `git checkout` and ABORTS the bisect. The script must `git checkout --\n  Cargo.lock` before returning its verdict.\n- `/usr/bin/time -p` writes to stderr: `2\u003e/dev/null` on the program silently\n  discards the timing and every commit returns \"skip\" — a bisect that tests\n  NOTHING and reports success.\n\n\n`fix(compiler): preserve SIMD dot signedness across backends` — SIMD4i32.hx,\nc_backend, cranelift_backend, instruction_lowering (+148), llvm_jit_backend\n(+388), tiered_backend (+36), blade.rs, instructions.rs, mir_builder,\nruntime_mapping, systems.rs. Its own tests are named\n`test_cranelift_shift_float_pollution` and `test_llvm_bitcast_i32_i64`.\n\nRULED OUT so far: `coerce_basic_value_to_type` (new in that commit) is called\nONLY from atomic store/RMW/CAS paths, not hot code. The tiered_backend changes\nare env-gated tracing plus a `func_ptr == 0` skip in startup init — that skip is\nthe most promising lead for a tier-up failure.\n\nRelated: [[reference_macos_peak_footprint_is_the_metric]],\n[[bugs_loop_body_class_alloc_never_freed]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_mandelbrot_45x_tierup_regression` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}